**The long-chunk models lose about as much on a single page as on four, so the loss is not caused by reading many pages.** Removing the three distractor pages left the L1024 deficit unchanged. (L1024 and C256 are the finetunes whose first training stage used 1,024-token and 256-token chunks.)

## Done this wake
- Four document cells finished healthy. Each had 1,500 questions, all judged, none unparseable, at the 1,003,520-pixel budget. The `<MEM>` slot counts matched the input audit (median 119 per question).
- Result: L1024 minus C256, judge accuracy (higher is better), same questions in every row:

| input | `<MEM>` slots per question (8 tokens each) | seed 42 (95% CI) | seed 43 (95% CI) | uncompressed decoder, same questions |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 4 pages | 455 | −6.2 pt [−8.6, −3.9] | −4.3 pt [−6.7, −1.8] | .809 |
| answer page only | 119 | −6.7 pt [−8.9, −4.5] | −4.9 pt [−7.1, −2.5] | .841 |

- The answer-page-only result is significant at both seeds (McNemar test p < .001) and negative on every source. That decides half of the campaign's test: the loss is not in multi-page reading.
- I wrote the page-transcription probe for the new models. It measures how many of a page's words can be rebuilt from its `<MEM>`s. It extends the old probe to models that spread each `<MEM>` over 8 positions, and adds a scoring script with paired statistics. A file paired with itself gives 0, and the script does show a difference on two old recorded files. It has not run on a GPU yet.
- I updated `docs/longdoc_diagnosis.md` (bottleneck table plus a new table for these models) and `STATE.md`. I added a +1 to an existing expmon bug report: the display page claims "0 rows" while it actually holds 16.

## Running now
- Four cells test the other half: the model picks the answer page and that page goes in uncompressed, while the other three stay compressed. They run on all four finetunes, about 30 min each plus judging.
- If the gap closes to within ±2 pt at both seeds, the loss sits in how L1024 compresses a page.

## Next wake (starts when these four cells finish, at most 3 h)
1. Pair the four new cells.
2. Check the new probe first: on the old model it must rebuild 70.5% of page words, ±2 pt.
3. Then compare word recall between the two stage-2 models (the ones before finetuning), on 501 pages.
4. Run plain ten-passage text on the four finetunes.

Nothing needed from you.
